<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The unit suite of ADR-0060's taxonomy, which design 20 section 3.1 row 1
    describes as "domain logic and handlers in isolation, no container".

    THE PROJECT NAME IS A CHOICE WITH NO SOURCE. Searched 2026-08-08 at commit
    c66a2da with `git grep -c "UnitTests"` over all tracked files, a plain
    substring that over-counts and never under-counts: zero hits. THAT SEARCH
    CANNOT RETURN ZERO AGAIN, because this file is now a hit; the commit is named
    so the reading stays checkable. The taxonomy names a project
    for one row only, the architecture one, and that name comes from ADR-0024
    rather than from the taxonomy. The Unit row names a tool and an owning ADR
    and no project. So this name follows the assembly convention of ADR-0065
    (everything under `Nami.Identity.*`) and the shape of the one sibling that
    exists, and it is recorded here rather than presented as sourced.

    TargetFramework reads $(NamiApplicationTargetFramework), the SINGLE-target
    knob, for the reason `Nami.Identity.ArchitectureTests` records: ADR-0030
    parameter B splits the knobs into published library and host, a test project
    is neither, and under the testing platform this project genuinely is an
    executable. Both knobs read `net10.0` today, so the choice first becomes
    visible when .NET 12 ships.

    OutputType is Exe and TestingPlatformDotnetTestSupport is set because
    xunit.v3 drives Microsoft Testing Platform itself. THE PROPERTY IS NOT
    OPTIONAL: a test project that omits it is SKIPPED by `dotnet test` rather
    than failing it, so a suite that runs nothing looks exactly like a suite
    that passes. Measured on 2026-08-08 by reading the count rather than the
    word: `dotnet test Nami.Identity.slnx` reports one line per assembly, and
    the new line reads "Passed: 15" beside the architecture suite's "Passed: 2".
    There is no combined total to read, which is the second half of why the
    word Passed is not the thing to check.

    There is one PackageReference and it carries no Version attribute. Central
    Package Management is on, so a version here is NU1008 and a missing
    PackageVersion row is NU1010. The xunit.v3 row already exists in
    Directory.Packages.props, so this project adds no package and owes no new
    licence read under ADR-0026. Confirmed at the graph rather than assumed:
    read from this project's own obj/project.assets.json on 2026-08-08, the
    net10.0 target holds sixteen packages plus the one project reference, and
    each of the sixteen appears at that exact version in section 3.1 of
    docs/DEPENDENCY-LICENSES.md, which enumerated twenty-three for the
    architecture project. All seven this project does not take are reachable
    from TngTech.ArchUnitNET.xUnitV3, walked in that project's own assets file
    on the same date rather than concluded by subtraction.

    Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk and xunit.runner.visualstudio are both absent on
    purpose, as they are in the sibling project. There is no assertion library
    either: ADR-0060 settled on 2026-08-02 that assertions come from what
    xunit.v3.assert ships.

    There are no PublicAPI.*.txt files here and there must not be. ADR-0044
    parameter A locks the surface of every public package; this is not one, and
    IsPackable is false.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>$(NamiApplicationTargetFramework)</TargetFramework>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <TestingPlatformDotnetTestSupport>true</TestingPlatformDotnetTestSupport>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    CA1707 ("Identifiers should not contain underscores") is exempted here, and
    the exemption is per-project because a directory-wide one is forbidden.

    WHAT COLLIDES. ADR-0060 requires tests "named and structured as scenarios,
    in Given / When / Then form" (`0060:61`), and ADR-0065:88 restates it as
    this repository's test-naming rule. Written with the three parts visible, a
    name carries underscores. ADR-0094 sets AnalysisMode to Recommended, and
    CA1707 is inside that set, so under the solution-wide TreatWarningsAsErrors
    every such name is a build error. Measured 2026-08-08 over fifteen test
    methods: fifteen distinct CA1707 errors, and the same build with
    -p:AnalysisMode=Default produced none, which matches the rule's own page
    listing it as not enabled by default in .NET 10.

    WHY THIS MECHANISM AND NOT AN .editorconfig SECTION. ADR-0093 parameter B is
    explicit: "No carve-out for `tests/` ... a warning suppressed by directory
    is a suppression nobody re-reads. Where a specific test genuinely needs a
    warning, parameter D is the route" (`0093:94-98`). ADR-0094:90-91 imports
    that same reasoning onto the analyzer-breadth axis. Parameter D is this: "a
    per-project `<NoWarn>` with a comment ... that says which diagnostic, why,
    and what would let it be removed" (`0093:133-136`). A `[tests/**/*.cs]`
    section in .editorconfig was written first and reverted on that evidence,
    because it would have pre-authorised the suppression for every future test
    suite without any of them opting in.

    WHY IT IS SAFE HERE. The rule's own page says so: "Do not suppress warnings
    for production code. However, it's safe to suppress this warning for test
    code." Read 2026-08-08 on the CA1707 page under
    learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/fundamentals/code-analysis/quality-rules
    (unwrapped, so it can be pasted). Its rule description is about libraries
    other people consume, and nothing here is packed. The scope is the project
    rather than the rule's own api_surface option, because that option filters
    by accessibility and a test method is public, so it cannot express "test
    code" at all.

    WHAT WOULD LET IT BE REMOVED. A change to ADR-0060's naming clause that
    stops the three parts being separated. The names satisfy ADR-0060 in
    PascalCase too, so this exemption buys legibility rather than compliance.

    THE SCOPE IS WIDER THAN METHOD NAMES. CA1707 also reaches namespaces, type
    names, and parameters. That is accepted rather than intended, because the
    option that would narrow it does not exist.

    PART 7 OF scripts/test-warnings-as-errors.sh WATCHES THE PropertyGroup
    BELOW, and it has two halves because a property read alone was not enough.
    Review broke the first version four ways on 2026-08-08, each leaving the
    script green while CA1707 was off for src/: an .editorconfig severity line, a
    WarningsNotAsErrors entry, a NoWarn written with one space after the
    semicolon, and a tests/Directory.Build.props. Three of the four never touch
    NoWarn. So 7a builds a probe INSIDE src/ and asserts the compiler still
    reports CA1707 there, and 7b reads this property and pins the number of
    exempt projects to exactly one. All five breaks, plus deleting the property
    below, were replanted against the fixed script and each one fails.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1707</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The ProjectReference gives the tests the types to exercise. The arrow points
    from the test into Abstractions and never the other way, so design 01
    section 3.1's "Abstractions depends on nothing" is untouched by it.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/Nami.Identity.Abstractions/Nami.Identity.Abstractions.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
